31,2,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Ryuji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Midori,,"My findings are concerning. Last winter's outage was unowned for two days because support was scattered across four suppliers. Also, some depots abandoned standard rollout procedures almost immediately to return to their own routines.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"My findings confirm my fears. We have no freeze window during peak season, and a previous attempt at a cutover caused a total collapse to paper dispatch. Furthermore, our yard hardware is a fragmented mess of old models.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"It's just as I feared. We've got a dispatch tool that's basically a fossil because we're too scared to update it, and a 2018 data migration that was a total hack job by outsiders. We're just layering patches on ruins.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The situation is worse than I thought. We are losing half our system knowledge to retirement, and it takes nearly a year to fill those roles. Our staff are already stretched too thin to absorb a difficult transition.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are all uncovering a rather bleak tapestry. My findings suggest a systemic blindness to long-term costs; while capital requests are scrutinized, the recurring operating expenses—the ""silent"" bills—creep upward virtually unchecked.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Ryuji @May, it sounds like we're facing a perfect storm of technical debt and operational chaos. If we don't find a vendor who provides a single point of accountability, we'll just repeat that two-day outage.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, exactly. But accountability isn't enough. We need a vendor whose system can actually run on our outdated hardware and a deployment plan that respects the peak-season reality, or we'll be back to paper.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, relying on a vendor to ""fit"" our junk hardware is a trap. If we just buy something that works with fossils, we're just buying a newer fossil. We need a clean break and a migration that actually works.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@May, a clean break sounds ideal, but who will manage it? With our administrators retiring and hiring taking nearly a year, we don't have the internal bandwidth for a high-friction migration. We need heavy vendor support.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, while heavy support is necessary, we must be wary of the price tag attached to that ""help."" If we outsource our competence, we risk a permanent, escalating subscription to a vendor's benevolence.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I understand the cost concern, but the cost of a two-day total outage is higher. We need a balance: a vendor who takes ownership of the system's stability without making us permanently dependent.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@May, a ""clean break"" is a luxury we can't afford if it ignores the yard. If the software requires hardware we can't deploy instantly, the system is useless. We need pragmatic compatibility, not just a new version of a fossil.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, pragmatism is great, but let's not confuse it with laziness. If we keep accommodating our mess, we'll never leave it. I want a vendor who forces us to modernize, not one who enables our decay.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@May, forcing modernization on a staff that is already exhausted and understaffed is a recipe for failure. If the system is too complex to adopt, the crews will just ignore it, like they did in the last rollout.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, @Misaki, we are debating the ""how"" while ignoring the ""how much."" Whether we modernize or accommodate, we must avoid a pricing model where the ""support"" becomes a bottomless pit of monthly add-ons. Predictability is our only shield.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Midori,,"It seems we are torn between modernization and stability. To avoid another outage or a rejected rollout, we need a partner who offers a single point of contact and a flexible implementation that respects our staff's limits.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. We must prioritize a vendor that understands the physical reality of the depot. If the solution doesn't work with our current hardware and our peak-season schedule, it's a failure regardless of its technical sophistication.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"I'll play along with the ""pragmatism,"" but let's be clear: if we pick a vendor that just patches over our ruins, we're just delaying the inevitable. I want a solution that actually lasts, not another temporary fix.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I just want to ensure we don't break the people who keep this company running. We need a vendor that provides direct, hands-on assistance to the crews, not just a manual and a help desk.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a delicate dance, isn't it? We seek a savior who is technically robust, operationally gentle, and physically compatible, yet we must ensure this savior doesn't charge us for the air we breathe once the contract is signed.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
